      "thinking": "The real fault is that the pricing service call has no timeout or deadline, so the shop waits indefinitely on a slow dependency, dragging down io-shop's latency by design\u2014it waits and reports how long it waited rather than retrying or pricing the basket itself. I need to figure out what fault we can actually defend against, since the investigation shows it arrived through this dependency, and make the code resilient to that scenario recurring.\n\nI'm checking whether bounding the call with a timeout could let the page degrade gracefully\u2014since basket_total_cents is optional, the page could render without it and report a pricing delay instead of failing outright. I'm also scanning rollout.py and other files for existing timeout constants, and reviewing the memory metrics (2GiB limit, 467MB used) to rule out a resource issue\u2014everything there looks fine, pointing back to the missing timeout as the real defensible fault.\n\nThe real problem is that the pricing seam's Callable[[str], PricingAnswer] signature has no way to impose a timeout without changing the interface, which would ripple to callers like tests and account_page. I'm weighing whether to add a timeout parameter to AskThePricingService or introduce a budget check against took_ms, though the latter doesn't actually save latency since the time's already spent \u2014 so I'm now looking at payment_provider to see if it already implements something like a circuit breaker or timeout pattern I could reuse.\n\n",
